om_rgbic Firmware Flashed to Bench K1 — Dual-Channel 138-LED Clocked RGBIC Rig\\n79296 \\\" ✓ Device Registry Prior Bench Row Marked Superseded After k1_custom_rgbic Flash\\n79299 4:53a ⚖ New Custom Build Environment Planned for WS2815 RGBIC LED Channels\\n79300 \\\" ● WS2815 Protocol Corrected from APA102 to Clockless Single-Wire in K1_CUSTOM_RGBIC_V1\\nS10905 Implement and flash new K1_CUSTOM_RGBIC_V1 build environment for WS2815 dual-channel LED strips — completed, verified, and live on bench K1 (Jul 12 at 4:54 AM)\\nS10906 Color order correction for WS2815 dual-channel build — both channels updated from RGB to GRB and reflashing in progress (Jul 12 at 4:54 AM)\\n79301 4:55a ● WS2815 Primary Channel Color Order Corrected from RGB to GRB\\n79302 4:56a ● WS2815 Secondary Channel Color Order Corrected from RGB to GRB\\nS10907 WS2815 GRB build successfully flashed to bench K1 — awaiting eyes-on color verification to confirm or diagnose remaining color order issue (Jul 12 at 4:56 AM)\\nS10909 12V 5050 LED strip (Data + CLK) wiring with ESP32-S3 and external 12V PSU — common ground requirement (Jul 12 at 4:57 AM)\\n79309 5:15a ○ 12V 5050 LED Strip Wiring with ESP32-S3 and External Power Supply\\n79310 5:19a ● Breakthrough Fix Achieved After Extended Debugging\\n79311 5:20a ● Root Cause Found: LED Corruption from Missing Common Ground on 12V Strip\\n79312 5:21a ✓ config_types.h Comment Updated to Reflect WS2812B Driver Resolution\\n79313 \\\" ✓ constants.h Clock-Pin Comment Updated: CLK Wires Carry Mirrored Data, Not SPI Clock\\n79314 \\\" ✓ globals.h SECONDARY_LED_COUNT Comment Updated to WS2812B Protocol\\n79315 \\\" ✓ CHANGELOG.md Updated with Eyes-On Pass and Root Cause for k1_custom_rgbic\\n**79316** 5:22a ✓ **Device-Build-Registry Updated: k1_custom_rgbic Status Flipped from PENDING to EYES-ON PASS**\\nThe device-build-registry.md is the project's canonical hardware state document — it must be updated on every flash. This edit closes out the k1_custom_rgbic bring-up by recording the eyes-on pass status with the Captain's confirmation quote, the root cause of the corruption saga (missing common ground), and the two reverted dead-ends. The prior bench state (k1_bench_im73d) is demoted to \\\"superseded\\\" in the table to reflect that the bench K1 is currently running the custom 12V rig firmware. This completes the 6-file documentation sweep triggered by the hardware fix.\\n~452t ⚒ 7,746\\n\\n**79317** 5:23a ⚖ **New Custom Build Environment Planned for WS2815 RGB LED Channels**\\nThe primary session received a request to architect a new custom build environment for an embedded firmware project. This environment is not related to the existing K1 Firmware product. The core hardware change is a migration from WS2812 (2-wire, Data only) addressable RGB LEDs to WS2815 (3-wire, Data + Clock) addressable RGB LEDs — sometimes called RGBICs. Each of the 2 LED channels changes from 160 WS2812 units to 138 WS2815 units. The existing Data GPIO pin assignments from the \\\"bench k1\\\" build environment are preserved, but two new GPIO pins (IO 7 for channel 1 clock, IO 8 for channel 2 clock) must be added to support the WS2815's additional clock line. The user directed the primary Claude to invoke a full suite of thinking frameworks (TRIZ, Cynefin, OODA, Map-Territory, Steel-Manning, Red-Team) and code analysis skills before beginning implementation, and to ask clarifying questions to ensure complete understanding of the requirements.\\n~487t ⚖ 8\\n\\n**79318** \\\" ○ **k1_custom_rgbic Build: Root Cause of LED Corruption Was Missing Common Ground**\\nThe k1_custom_rgbic build environment experienced persistent LED data corruption that took 4 reflashes to diagnose. Before finding the true root cause, two incorrect protocol hypotheses were tested: APA102/clocked-SPI (produced garbled output from pixel 0) and FastLED's WS2815 chipset driver (wrong timing constants — the strip requires 250ns/750ns which WS2812B provides). The actual problem was electrical, not firmware: the strip runs from an external 12V PSU whose GND was not tied to the ESP32-S3 GND, meaning the data signal had no shared 0V reference with the microcontroller and was interpreted as random noise. Fixing the common ground resolved all corruption. The breakthrough came from consulting the Pixelblaze screenshot showing the Captain's working configuration, which provided exact ground-truth protocol parameters. A 74AHCT125 level shifter on DATA/CLK lines is noted as a contingency if 3.3V logic levels prove marginal for the 12V strip's data input threshold.\\n~535t ⌕ 5,375\\n\\n**79319** \\\" ◆ **k1_custom_rgbic Environment Implemented: 2×138 WS2815-Compatible Strip on ESP32-S3**\\nThe k1_custom_rgbic environment is a custom bench rig that is explicitly NOT the K1 product. It replaces the stock 2×160 WS2812B LED channels with two independent 138-LED 12V 5050 addressable strips. The environment extends k1_bench_im73d_ble, inheriting IM73D PDM microphone and BLE-MIDI capabilities. Data GPIO pins are preserved from the bench k1 configuration (GPIO4 primary, GPIO5 secondary). Since WS2815 LEDs have a 3-wire interface (Data + Clock), GPIO7 and GPIO8 are added as clock outputs — GPIO8 was reclaimed from the `RNG_SEED_PIN` constant which was confirmed to be dead and never read. The CLK wire on the WS2815 strips is mirror-driven with the same buffer as the DATA signal. The native resolution remains 160 LEDs in software, with `scale_to_strip()` performing the 160→138 downsampling at render time. The feature flag architecture ensures zero impact on all other build environments when K1_CUSTOM_RGBIC_V1 is not defined. A new memory file was created and registered in the project MEMORY.md index.\\n~541t ⚒ 5,375\\n\\nS10910 Implement new custom build environment k1_custom_rgbic for 2×138 WS2815 12V-5050 LED strips on bench K1 ([unverified commit]) — replacing stock 2×160 WS2812B channels with different LED type and adding clock GPIOs (Jul 12 at 5:24 AM)\\n### Jul 13, 2026\\n**79970** 9:16p ○ **Codex Adversarial Review Retry Triggered for lane/gem-port-beat-pulse**\\nA previous subagent was tasked with running a Codex adversarial review on the SpectraSynq K1 Firmware repository (branch `lane/gem-port-beat-pulse`) but timed out during a PING check without completing. A retry was issued using the codex-companion CLI (v1.0.6) in background/resume-last mode. The task directive instructs the agent to first assess the working tree for uncommitted changes to three specific files, then run an adversarial review scoped only to product diff `7b3bb99`. After resolving only verified/source-grounded findings, the agent must rerun pytest and the PIO hardware build, record the firmware binary SHA256 and size, update the deployment ledger row #2 with actual Codex outcomes, and produce a Captain handoff document for modes 35/36 vs 32. Strict hard rules prohibit flashing hardware, creating new planning artifacts, or running optional pilot modes. The shell returned `{}` meaning the background task was accepted but results are pending.\\n~640t ⌕ 953\\n\\n\\nAccess 370k tokens of past work via get_observations([IDs]) or mem-search skill.\"},\"systemMessage\":\"\\u001b[1m\\u001b[36m[SpectraSynq_K1_Firmware/k1-ruflo-live] recent context, 2026-07-16 4:24am GMT+8\\u001b[0m\\n\\u001b[90m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\\u001b[0m\\n\\n\\u001b[2mLegend: session-request | ● bugfix | ◆ feature | ↻ refactor | ✓ change | ○ discovery | ⚖ decision | ⚠ security_alert | ⚷ security_note\\u001b[0m\\n\\n\\u001b[1mColumn Key\\u001b[0m\\n\\u001b[2m  Read: Tokens to read this observation (cost to learn it now)\\u001b[0m\\n\\u001b[2m  Work: Tokens spent on work that produced this record ( research, building, deciding)\\u001b[0m\\n\\n\\u001b[2mContext Index: This semantic index (titles, types, files, tokens) is usually sufficient to understand past work.\\u001b[0m\\n\\n\\u001b[2mWhen you need implementation details, rationale, or debug